<section xml:id="sec_electric-field" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Electric Field</title>
		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-elec-field">
	<title>Electric Field</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="D3uxD64rcGI"><description>Introducing the electric field.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>
	
<subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>



<definition>
	<title>
	Electric field
	</title>
		<statement>
		
			<p>
			
			</p>
			
			<p>
			The <term> electric field </term> is a <em> vector field </em> that influences the force that a charged object experiences.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric field is a vector.</alert>  Like other vectors, it has a magnitude and a direction.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric field has a value at every point in space.</alert>  Either the magnitude or the direction (or both) can be different at different points in space.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric field influences electric charge.</alert>  The motion of charged objects through space is governed by an electric force acting on the charged object by any pre-existing electric fields.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</statement>
</definition>

<definition>
	<title>
	Electric force
	</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
			
			</p>

			<p>
			The <term>electric force</term> on a point-like object (often called a test charge) is the product of the object's charge and the electric field at the location of the object: <me>\vec{F}^E = q_{\text{test}} \vec{E}</me>
			</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-gravity2">
      <title>Analogy with Gravity</title>
      <p>
        Like the gravitational force, the electric force is a non-contact force, as opposed to a contact force. The electric and gravitational fields are responsible for the force between charges and masses, respectively.  In fact, the mathematical structure of the forces are directly analogous: <me>\vec{F}^E = q_{\text{test}} \vec{E}</me><me>\vec{F}^g = m_{\text{test}} \vec{g}</me>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
	


	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<activity>
			<title>Fields in Science</title>
			<p>

			</p>
			  <p>An electric field is a <em>field</em>, which means it has a potentially different value everywhere in space.  Identify one other quantity you are familiar with that could be described as a field.  It can be from physics or from another science, or from your everyday experience.  Explain why you believe the quantity you chose is a field.</p>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<title>Sensemaking: Units</title>
			<statement>
			<p>

			</p>
			  <p>What are the units of the electric field?</p>
				</statement>
				<answer>
					<p>
						N/C
					</p>
				</answer>
		</activity>
		
		<activity>
			<title>How Flowers Talk to Bees</title>
			<p>

			</p>
